# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the sanitized corpus on Linux CI
# (scripts/ci-test-plan.mjs). One `leak:<function>` line per intentional
# leak, under a comment that gives the reason. Empty: no corpus program
# leaks.

# Node islands (scope-B NI-7c): OpenSSL inside libnode is initialized with
# OPENSSL_INIT_NO_ATEXIT (scr_node_bridge.cc, KeepOpenSSLAtExit), so its
# process-lifetime state is never freed: OPENSSL_cleanup at exit raced the
# threads Node owns that can still be inside OpenSSL.
leak:OPENSSL_init_crypto
# Node islands (scope-B NI-7c): Node's root certificates (bundled, system
# store, NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS) live in function-local statics that Node's
# per-process teardown frees; a scriptc program leaves that teardown to
# process exit (scr_node_bridge.cc, Stop), and the vectors' own destructors
# drop the last pointers at exit.
leak:node::crypto::LoadCACertificates
leak:node::crypto::LoadSystemCACertificates
leak:node::crypto::GetBundledRootCertificates
leak:node::crypto::GetSystemStoreCACertificates
leak:node::crypto::GetExtraCACertificates
leak:node::crypto::GetOpenSSLSystemCertificates
leak:node::crypto::LoadCertsFromBIO
# The same certificates under a stripped libnode (the packs the
# libnode-packs workflow builds), where the loading threads' Node frames
# have no names: each one is parsed through libnode's OpenSSL PEM reader.
# The runtime has no OpenSSL of its own, so only libnode reaches it.
leak:PEM_ASN1_read_bio
# The certificates' lazily decoded keys, cached in them when a root store
# is built.
leak:node::crypto::NewRootCertStore
# The same policy covers OpenSSL's own default-verify-path store, which a
# TLS context created inside an island loads on its own: undici needs TLS
# for the builtin classes it backs (Headers, Request, Response), and OpenSSL
# then parses the system CA bundle through PEM/X509 with no node::crypto
# frame in the stack, so the entries above cannot match. That state is
# likewise freed only by OPENSSL_cleanup at exit, which NI-7c keeps off, so
# it is named here by OpenSSL's allocator entry points instead.
leak:CRYPTO_malloc
leak:CRYPTO_zalloc
